1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:00,998
Listen.

2
00:00:01,018 --> 00:00:04,106
A violin begins a phrase in Philadelphia.

3
00:00:04,126 --> 00:00:06,596
A cello answers from Illinois.

4
00:00:07,510 --> 00:00:12,989
The musicians are thirteen hundred kilometres apart, playing the same piece, live.

5
00:00:13,009 --> 00:00:16,813
Each has to hear the other in time for the next note.

6
00:00:16,930 --> 00:00:21,472
How did an American university network make that possible?

7
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:23,704
Go back to 1996.

8
00:00:23,724 --> 00:00:25,628
The web was growing fast.

9
00:00:25,648 --> 00:00:33,372
On American campuses, researchers wanted to do things that ordinary connections struggled to support.

10
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:44,240
A scientist might need to send a large dataset to another university, use a powerful computer there, and get the results back while an experiment was still running.

11
00:00:44,290 --> 00:00:50,560
That October, thirty-four American research universities launched a joint initiative.

12
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:52,680
They called it Internet2.

13
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:58,222
In 1999, a new national backbone called Abilene went live.

14
00:00:58,242 --> 00:01:01,452
Regional networks linked campuses to it.

15
00:01:02,390 --> 00:01:07,180
Its early links carried about two and a half gigabits per second.

16
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:10,188
By 2004, that figure had reached ten.

17
00:01:10,570 --> 00:01:13,357
Then came a different kind of advance.

18
00:01:13,377 --> 00:01:20,496
In 2007, researchers gained the ability to reserve a dedicated connection for a limited time.

19
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:27,012
A team could arrange the capacity it needed for the hours its experiment would run.

20
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:29,790
Now listen to the duet again.

21
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:33,880
For a musician, even a small delay can change the rhythm.

22
00:01:33,930 --> 00:01:44,560
In 2012, a violinist and a cellist used Internet2 and low-latency audio and video technology to perform together across those thirteen hundred kilometres.

23
00:01:44,610 --> 00:01:49,670
You could hear the quality of the connection in the music itself.

24
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:59,200
By 2022, Internet2 had moved its traffic onto a new generation of backbone links carrying four hundred gigabits per second.

25
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:02,463
The next planned upgrade would double that capacity.

26
00:02:02,483 --> 00:02:10,008
Through partners such as Europe’s GÉANT, a campus in the United States can now reach research teams around the world.

27
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:14,522
Thirty-four universities set out to share computers and data.

28
00:02:14,542 --> 00:02:21,678
Along the way, they made it possible to share something as fleeting as the next note in a duet.
